# The background scene: work off the app thread, posted back
# (DESIGN.md's threading model; docs/background-work-plan.md).
#
# THE DESIGN PRINCIPLE, and why the script looks like this: a wrong
# implementation must DEADLOCK, not merely disagree. The worker parks
# until a CLICK releases it, and only a live app thread can process a
# click — so a binding that let background work occupy the app thread
# cannot reach the end of this file at all. It could not even deliver
# its own release. That is much stronger than reading back a different
# value, and it is what stops the scene passing for an implementation
# that simply blocked.
#
# NO NEW VERBS, deliberately: `click` and `expect` carry all of it, and
# every new verb costs an arm in BOTH interpreters.
expect label#0 "idle"
click button#0
# The worker has started and PARKED. Nothing is posted yet.
expect label#0 "working"

# THE CLAIM. The worker is parked with nothing posted, and the app
# thread must still be serving input. On a binding that blocked, this
# click is never processed and the scene dies here instead of later.
click button#1
expect label#1 "alive"

# And the posts have NOT landed yet. This separates a real background
# post from a guest that computed everything eagerly on the app thread
# and only pretended to park — that guest arrives here already showing
# the final value.
expect label#0 "working"

# Release. Three posts, and the accumulator makes this a test of ORDER
# rather than of which one happened to run last: a stack instead of a
# queue reads "321", not "123".
click button#2
expect label#0 "123"

# A post from INSIDE a transaction QUEUES for after; it never nests.
# The handler appends a, posts a closure appending b, appends c — so it
# commits "ac" and the posted closure then commits "acb". Nesting runs
# the closure between them and can only ever produce "abc". The two
# strings are unreachable from each other, which makes this the one
# discriminator a scene can express: thread identity is not observable
# from here, so "did the post run inline" has to be asked this way.
click button#3
expect label#2 "acb"

# One REAL-TREE read at the end. Every assertion above reads kaya's own
# model and would pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing.
expect_ax label#2 "label/acb"
